__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"HANDWRITTEN_QUESTIONS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return s}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"QUESTIONS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return o}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"questionById",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return c}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"isGenerated",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return h}});var t=r(d[0]);const n=(t,n,s,o,l,c,h,f)=>({id:t,level:n,topic:s,prompt:o,choices:l,answer:c,explanation:h,reference:f}),s=[n("d01","dispatch","dispatch","What is the FIRST piece of information a telecommunicator should obtain and verify on a 911 call?",["The caller's name","The location of the emergency","The nature of the emergency","The callback number"],1,"Location comes first. If the call drops, responders can still be sent to the right place.","APCO/NENA call-taking standards"),n("d02","dispatch","dispatch","In Enhanced 911, what do ANI and ALI provide?",["Agency and incident numbers","Automatic Number Identification and Automatic Location Identification","Alarm notification and incident logging","Apparatus number and location index"],1,"ANI shows the caller's number and ALI shows the location tied to it, both pulled automatically from the 911 database.","NENA E9-1-1 standards"),n("d03","dispatch","dispatch","Wireless Phase II 911 data gives the telecommunicator:",["Only the cell tower sector","The caller's approximate latitude/longitude","The subscriber's billing address","The caller's medical history"],1,"Phase I gives the tower/sector. Phase II adds the caller's estimated coordinates.","FCC wireless E911 rules"),n("d04","dispatch","dispatch","A caller reports a house fire and is still inside. The correct pre-arrival instruction is:",["Try to find the fire extinguisher","Get everyone out, stay out, and do not go back in","Open all windows to let the smoke out","Stay on the line inside until units arrive"],1,"Get out and stay out. Re-entry kills civilians. If they're trapped, tell them to close doors, seal gaps, and signal from a window.","NFPA 1225 / fire pre-arrival protocols"),n("d05","dispatch","gas","A caller smells strong natural gas inside the home. You should advise them to:",["Find and shut off the gas at the stove","Open windows and turn on exhaust fans","Leave immediately without operating switches, and call from outside","Light a candle to check for a leak"],2,"Electrical switches, phones, and flames inside are ignition sources. Evacuate first, then talk from a safe distance.","Utility/fire gas-odor protocols"),n("d06","dispatch","dispatch","A CO alarm is sounding and occupants report headaches and nausea. The best handling is:",["Advise them to reset the detector","Treat it as an emergency: evacuate occupants and send fire and EMS","Send only the utility company","Schedule a non-emergency inspection"],1,"Symptoms mean CO exposure is likely. Get people into fresh air and send both fire and EMS.","Fire/EMS dispatch protocols"),n("d07","dispatch","ems","Telephone CPR instructions for an adult should direct compressions at a rate of:",["60\u201380 per minute","80\u2013100 per minute","100\u2013120 per minute","130\u2013150 per minute"],2,"The current AHA guideline is 100\u2013120 compressions per minute for adults.","AHA Guidelines for CPR & ECC"),n("d08","dispatch","mayday","A firefighter transmits a MAYDAY. The dispatcher's priority is to:",["Ask the firefighter to switch to another channel","Make sure the IC acknowledges it, alert units, and give the Mayday radio priority","Page out off-duty members first","Wait for the IC to request help"],1,"Dispatch makes sure the Mayday was heard and acknowledged, restricts routine traffic, and supports the IC's resource requests.","NFPA 1561 / local Mayday SOG"),n("d09","dispatch","dispatch","Why do dispatchers make move-up or cover assignments during large incidents?",["To give crews a break","To keep station coverage for the rest of the town","To stage units at the fire","To satisfy mutual aid billing"],1,"When local units are committed, moving units into empty station areas keeps response times reasonable for new calls.","Dispatch resource management principles"),n("d10","dispatch","dispatch","When there is a conflict, which radio communication method does NIMS require?",["10-codes","Agency-specific signals","Plain language","Phonetic codes only"],2,"NIMS requires plain language so that multiple agencies understand each other.","NIMS Communications & Information Management"),n("d11","dispatch","marine","A caller sees someone struggling in the water off the marina dock. Pre-arrival instructions should include:",["Swim out to the victim immediately","Keep eyes on the victim, throw something that floats, and do not enter the water","Call the victim's family","Drive to the nearest bridge"],1,"Reach, throw, row, go. Untrained rescuers who enter the water often become victims. Keeping a visual on the victim gives responders a last-seen point.","Water rescue awareness / NFPA 1006"),n("d12","dispatch","systems","Under most SOGs, if an alarm company calls to cancel after units are already responding, the dispatcher should:",["Cancel all units immediately","Notify responding units and let the officer decide whether to downgrade or continue","Ignore the cancellation","Upgrade to a working fire"],1,"A cancellation is passed along to units. The responding officer usually decides whether to continue (often one unit non-emergency) or clear.","Typical alarm-response SOG"),n("d13","dispatch","dispatch","In CAD, the Emergency Service Zone (ESZ) for an address determines:",["The caller's cell carrier","Which fire, EMS, and police agencies respond to that location","The billing rate","The hydrant flow"],1,"The ESZ/ESN links an address to the agencies responsible for it, which drives run-card recommendations.","NENA / CAD fundamentals"),n("d14","dispatch","mva","MVA caller says a victim is conscious but complaining of neck pain. The caller should be told to:",["Help the victim walk to the curb","Not move the victim unless there is immediate danger such as fire","Remove the victim's helmet","Give the victim water"],1,"Leave possible spinal injuries alone unless the scene itself becomes life-threatening.","EMD pre-arrival instructions"),n("d15","dispatch","wildland","On a brush fire call, which caller information most affects your resource decision?",["The caller's age","Wind conditions and nearby exposures (homes)","What time the caller noticed the smell","The color of the smoke only"],1,"Wind drives spread and nearby structures add life hazard. Both can justify upgrading the response.","Wildland dispatch guidelines"),n("f101","ff1","behavior","Which choice correctly lists the stages of compartment fire development?",["Ignition, flashover, backdraft, decay","Incipient, growth, fully developed, decay","Smoldering, flaming, rollover, collapse","Incipient, free-burning, smoldering, cooling"],1,"IFSTA's stages are incipient, growth, fully developed, and decay. Flashover is the transition between growth and fully developed.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Fire Behavior"),n("f102","ff1","behavior","Flashover typically occurs when the upper gas layer reaches approximately:",["500\xb0F (260\xb0C)","1,100\xb0F (593\xb0C)","1,800\xb0F (982\xb0C)","2,500\xb0F (1,371\xb0C)"],1,"Radiant heat from a gas layer around 1,100\xb0F (593\xb0C) brings every exposed fuel in the room to its ignition temperature.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Fire Behavior"),n("f103","ff1","behavior","Which is a classic warning sign of a possible backdraft?",["Bright, visible flames at the windows","Thin white smoke at ground level","Pulsing smoke drawn back into openings, smoke-stained windows, little visible flame","A rapid drop in interior temperature"],2,"A ventilation-limited fire with heated fuel waiting for oxygen shows pulsing or 'breathing' smoke, darkened windows, and little visible flame.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Fire Behavior"),n("f104","ff1","behavior","The fire tetrahedron adds which element to the fire triangle?",["Pressure","Smoke","Uninhibited chemical chain reaction","Moisture"],2,"The tetrahedron is heat, fuel, oxygen, and a self-sustaining chemical chain reaction.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Fire Behavior"),n("f105","ff1","behavior","Heat transferred through direct contact between materials is:",["Convection","Radiation","Conduction","Advection"],2,"Conduction moves heat through direct contact, for example along a steel beam or pipe.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Fire Behavior"),n("f106","ff1","scba","Under the current NFPA SCBA standard, the end-of-service-time indicator (low-air alarm) activates at:",["10% of rated pressure","25% of rated pressure","33% of rated pressure","50% of rated pressure"],2,"The low-air alarm activates at 33% of rated pressure. It means you are now breathing your reserve.","NFPA 1981 (now NFPA 1970)"),n("f107","ff1","scba","Good air management means a firefighter plans to:",["Use the reserve air to finish the task","Leave the IDLH atmosphere before the low-air alarm activates","Leave only when the PASS sounds","Buddy-breathe when air is low"],1,"Under NFPA air-management practice, the low-air alarm is an emergency signal and not a reminder to start leaving. Your exit should already be planned.","NFPA 1404 / IFSTA Essentials \u2014 SCBA"),n("f108","ff1","scba","A PASS device's pre-alert begins sounding while you are still conscious. You should:",["Remove the PASS","Move to reset it","Ignore it until full alarm","Turn off your SCBA"],1,"The PASS pre-alert senses lack of motion. Moving resets it before it goes to full alarm.","NFPA 1982 / IFSTA Essentials \u2014 SCBA"),n("f109","ff1","search","The PRIMARY search is best described as:",["A slow, thorough search after the fire is under control","A fast but systematic search of the most likely victim locations","A search done only by RIC","An exterior-only search"],1,"Primary search is quick and systematic during operations. Secondary search is slow and thorough after the fire is controlled.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Search & Rescue"),n("f110","ff1","search","During an oriented or wall search, firefighters maintain orientation mainly by:",["Staying in contact with a wall, using a consistent right- or left-hand pattern","Counting steps","Following the sound of the pump","Looking for light"],0,"Keeping contact with the wall in one direction lets you retrace your path out.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Search & Rescue"),n("f111","ff1","ventilation","Ventilation that is NOT coordinated with fire attack is most likely to:",["Cool the structure","Increase fire growth along a new flow path","Have no effect","Extinguish the fire"],1,"UL/NIST research shows that adding air to a ventilation-limited fire without water on it causes rapid fire growth.","UL FSRI / IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Ventilation"),n("f112","ff1","ventilation","For positive-pressure ventilation (PPV), the blower is positioned:",["Inside the fire room","Outside the entry opening so the cone of air covers the doorway","On the roof","At the exhaust opening blowing inward"],1,"The fan sits outside so its cone of air seals the entry opening. Only start it after an exhaust opening is made.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Ventilation"),n("f113","ff1","ventilation","A vertical ventilation opening should ideally be cut:",["Over the exit stairs","As directly over the fire as safely possible","Upwind of the fire","At the eave edge"],1,"An opening directly over the seat of the fire lets heat and smoke rise out most efficiently.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Ventilation"),n("f114","ff1","hose","IFSTA classifies large-diameter hose (LDH) as hose with a diameter of:",["2 inches or larger","2\xbd inches or larger","3\xbd inches or larger","6 inches or larger"],2,"LDH is 3\xbd inches and larger. It is used mainly for supply lines.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Fire Hose"),n("f115","ff1","hose","Water hammer is most often caused by:",["Opening a hydrant too slowly","Closing a nozzle or valve too quickly","Kinks in LDH","Using a fog nozzle"],1,"Stopping flowing water suddenly sends a pressure surge back through the system. Open and close valves slowly.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Fire Hose"),n("f116","ff1","water","Under NFPA 291 color coding, a light-blue hydrant bonnet indicates a flow of:",["Less than 500 gpm","500\u2013999 gpm","1,000\u20131,499 gpm","1,500 gpm or more"],3,"Class AA (light blue) is 1,500+ gpm, Class A (green) is 1,000\u20131,499, Class B (orange) is 500\u2013999, and Class C (red) is under 500.","NFPA 291"),n("f117","ff1","water","A dry-barrel hydrant should be opened fully because a partially open hydrant may:",["Freeze immediately","Discharge through the open drain valve and undermine the hydrant","Increase static pressure","Cause cavitation in the main"],1,"When partly open, the drain valve stays open and water erodes the soil around the hydrant base.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Water Supply"),n("f118","ff1","ladders","The proper climbing angle for a ground ladder is approximately:",["60 degrees","65 degrees","75 degrees","85 degrees"],2,"About 75\xb0 gives the best balance of strength and stability. Use the angle marker or the 1:4 rule.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Ground Ladders"),n("f119","ff1","ladders","When a ground ladder is used for roof access, the tip should extend:",["Even with the gutter","One rung above the roof edge","Several rungs (about 3\u20135) above the roof edge","Ten feet above the roof"],2,"Extending several rungs above the edge gives a handhold, and the ladder is visible from the roof.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Ground Ladders"),n("f120","ff1","ladders","Before raising any ground ladder, the firefighter must check for:",["Wind direction only","Overhead obstructions, especially electrical wires","The building's age","Hydrant location"],1,"Overhead power lines are a leading cause of ladder electrocutions.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Ground Ladders"),n("f121","ff1","forcible","Before forcing entry through a door, firefighters should first:",["Cut the hinges","Check whether it's unlocked ('try before you pry')","Break the nearest window","Call for the irons"],1,"Many doors are unlocked. Checking first saves time and avoids damage.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Forcible Entry"),n("f122","ff1","forcible","The flat-head axe paired with a Halligan bar is commonly called:",["The rabbit tool","The irons","The K-tool set","The hook and can"],1,"'The irons' are the basic forcible entry tool set.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Forcible Entry"),n("f123","ff1","safety","The OSHA 'two-in/two-out' rule requires:",["Two engines on scene before any attack","At least two firefighters outside, properly equipped, when two enter an IDLH atmosphere","Two officers on every alarm","Two hoselines before entry"],1,"29 CFR 1910.134 requires a standby team outside. The one exception is a known life hazard where immediate action could prevent loss of life.","OSHA 29 CFR 1910.134 / NJ PEOSH"),n("f124","ff1","safety","Firefighters riding apparatus must be seated and belted:",["Only on highways","Whenever the vehicle is in motion","Only when responding emergency","Only in the cab"],1,"Members are seated and belted any time the apparatus moves.","NFPA 1500 (now NFPA 1550)"),n("f125","ff1","hazmat","In the Emergency Response Guidebook, the green-bordered pages provide:",["Guides by material name","Initial isolation and protective action distances","Placard images","Guides by ID number"],1,"Yellow pages list materials by ID number, blue by name, orange holds the guides, and green gives isolation and protective action distances.","DOT/PHMSA Emergency Response Guidebook"),n("f126","ff1","hazmat","When approaching a possible hazmat release, responders should approach from:",["Downwind and downhill","Upwind, uphill, and upstream","Any direction, quickly","Directly in line with the vapor cloud"],1,"Upwind, uphill, and upstream keeps vapors and runoff moving away from you.","IFSTA Hazmat Awareness / ERG"),n("f127","ff1","overhaul","Firefighters should keep SCBA on during overhaul because:",["It is required for photos","Carbon monoxide and toxic products of combustion remain at dangerous levels","Overhaul is always in zero visibility","It keeps their face shields clean"],1,"Overhaul atmospheres can hold high CO, HCN, and carcinogens even after the smoke clears.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Overhaul"),n("f128","ff1","construction","Which construction type is wood frame?",["Type I","Type II","Type IV","Type V"],3,"Type I is fire-resistive, Type II noncombustible, Type III ordinary, Type IV heavy timber, and Type V wood frame.","NFPA 220 / IFSTA Building Construction"),n("f129","ff1","construction","Under fire conditions, lightweight wood trusses and engineered I-joists can fail:",["Only after 30 minutes","In under 10 minutes, often with little warning","Never, if sprinkled","Only when wet"],1,"UL testing showed unprotected engineered floor systems collapsing within minutes of exposure.","UL FSRI / IFSTA Building Construction"),n("f130","ff1","extrication","Before any extrication tools are used, the vehicle must be:",["Towed","Stabilized with cribbing, step chocks, or struts","Rolled onto its roof","Washed down with foam"],1,"Stabilization stops the vehicle from moving, which protects both the patient and rescuers.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Vehicle Extrication"),n("f131","ff1","extrication","Using the 5-10-20 rule, how far should rescuers stay from an undeployed DRIVER frontal airbag?",["5 inches","10 inches","20 inches","36 inches"],1,"5 inches for side airbags, 10 for the driver frontal airbag, and 20 for the passenger frontal airbag.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Vehicle Extrication"),n("f132","ff1","gas","Propane (LPG) vapors tend to:",["Rise and dissipate quickly","Collect in low areas because LPG is heavier than air","Stay in the tank","Be odorless"],1,"Propane vapor density is about 1.5, so it pools in basements, ditches, and low spots. Natural gas is lighter than air.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Utilities / Hazmat"),n("f133","ff1","gas","When investigating a gas odor inside a building, firefighters should:",["Turn on lights to see better","Avoid creating ignition sources and meter the atmosphere","Open the gas meter to bleed the line","Use a cell phone inside to call the utility"],1,"Meter from the outside in, eliminate ignition sources, ventilate, and evacuate as readings require.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Utilities"),n("f134","ff1","wildland","The wildland safety acronym LCES stands for:",["Lookouts, Communications, Escape routes, Safety zones","Ladders, Crews, Engines, Staging","Locate, Contain, Extinguish, Secure","Lines, Command, Evacuation, Supply"],0,"LCES must be in place before engaging a wildland fire.","NWCG / IFSTA Wildland"),n("f135","ff1","elevator","Before working to open a stalled elevator car, rescuers must:",["Pry the hoistway doors immediately","Lock out / tag out the elevator's main power and communicate with occupants","Climb on the car roof","Call for a ladder truck to the roof"],1,"Shutting off the mainline disconnect prevents unexpected car movement. Reassure occupants while you work.","ASME A17.4 / IFSTA Technical Rescue"),n("f136","ff1","ems","For adult CPR, chest compressions should be at least how deep?",["1 inch","1\xbd inches","2 inches","3 inches"],2,"At least 2 inches (5 cm) but not more than 2.4 inches (6 cm) for adults.","AHA Guidelines for CPR & ECC"),n("f137","ff1","marine","Gasoline vapors in a boat's bilge are dangerous because they:",["Are lighter than air and vent away","Are heavier than air and accumulate in the low bilge","Cannot ignite on water","Are non-toxic"],1,"Gasoline vapor is heavier than air. Boats need blowers for this reason, and vapor-filled bilges can explode.","NFPA 302 / IFSTA Marine firefighting"),n("f201","ff2","ics","The five major functional areas of ICS under NIMS are:",["Command, Operations, Planning, Logistics, Finance/Administration","Command, Safety, Liaison, PIO, Staging","Fire, EMS, Police, Public Works, Health","Command, Rescue, Suppression, Ventilation, Overhaul"],0,"Command plus the four General Staff sections: Operations, Planning, Logistics, and Finance/Administration.","NIMS / NFPA 1561"),n("f202","ff2","investigation","Who is responsible for protecting evidence at a fire scene?",["Only the fire marshal","Only police","All firefighters operating on the scene","The insurance adjuster"],2,"Every member preserves evidence and notes observations such as conditions on arrival, open doors, and odors.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Fire Origin & Cause"),n("f203","ff2","investigation","A V-shaped burn pattern on a wall generally points toward:",["The exit","The area of origin","The ventilation opening","The last area to burn"],1,"V patterns spread upward and outward from a heat source. The low point often marks the area of origin.","NFPA 921 / IFSTA Essentials"),n("f204","ff2","hose","Smooth-bore handline nozzles are typically operated at a nozzle pressure of:",["50 psi","80 psi","100 psi","150 psi"],0,"Smooth-bore handlines run at 50 psi and master streams at 80 psi. Standard fog nozzles are 100 psi, low-pressure fog 50\u201375.","IFSTA Pumping Apparatus / Essentials"),n("f205","ff2","water","Friction loss in a hoseline increases significantly when:",["Flow (gpm) increases","Hose diameter increases","Elevation decreases","The hose is wet"],0,"Friction loss rises with the square of flow, so doubling gpm roughly quadruples friction loss.","IFSTA Hydraulics"),n("f206","ff2","water","On the fireground, elevation pressure in multistory buildings is commonly estimated as:",["1 psi per floor","5 psi per floor above the first","10 psi per floor","20 psi per floor"],1,"About 0.5 psi per foot, or roughly 5 psi per story.","IFSTA Hydraulics"),n("f207","ff2","hose","Which foam application suits polar solvents such as alcohols?",["Protein foam at 1%","Alcohol-resistant AFFF at the proportion for polar solvents (commonly 6% or per manufacturer)","Class A foam at 0.1%","Plain water fog"],1,"Polar solvents break down ordinary foam, so use AR foam at the manufacturer's polar-solvent proportion.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Foam"),n("f208","ff2","behavior","A 'transitional attack' is:",["Attacking from the interior, then exterior","Briefly applying water from outside into the fire compartment, then moving to interior attack","Changing from offensive to defensive","Rotating crews mid-fire"],1,"UL/NIST research supports cooling the fire compartment from outside before an interior push when conditions allow.","UL FSRI / IFSTA"),n("f209","ff2","ventilation","A 'flow path' is best defined as:",["The hose route from engine to fire","The volume between an inlet and an exhaust that lets heat and smoke move","The route victims take to exit","The water main grid"],1,"Heat and smoke move from high pressure to low pressure along the flow path. Firefighters should never be caught in the exhaust portion.","UL FSRI / IFSTA"),n("f210","ff2","systems","An OS&Y valve controlling a sprinkler system is OPEN when:",["The stem is flush with the wheel","The threaded stem is visibly extended","The handle is red","The valve is chained"],1,"Outside Screw & Yoke: a visible extended stem means open.","NFPA 25 / IFSTA Fire Protection Systems"),n("f211","ff2","systems","The most common type of automatic sprinkler system is the:",["Dry-pipe system","Deluge system","Wet-pipe system","Pre-action system"],2,"Wet-pipe systems hold water under pressure at the heads. They are simple and reliable in heated spaces.","NFPA 13 / IFSTA"),n("f212","ff2","systems","The Fire Department Connection (FDC) on a sprinklered building is used to:",["Drain the system","Supplement water supply and pressure to the sprinkler/standpipe system","Test the alarm","Connect a hydrant to the street"],1,"Engines pump into the FDC to support the system. Most SOGs make this an early priority.","NFPA 13/14 / IFSTA"),n("f213","ff2","mva","When approaching a vehicle fire, firefighters should approach:",["Directly from the front","From a 45\xb0 angle at the side, uphill/upwind when possible","Directly from the rear","From downhill"],1,"Bumpers, struts, and hatch cylinders can launch forward or backward. Approach from the corner and stay out of the path of fuel runoff.","IFSTA Essentials \u2014 Vehicle Fires"),n("f214","ff2","hazmat","Gross decontamination at a hazmat incident is performed to:",["Identify the chemical","Quickly remove most of the contaminant, usually by flushing with water","Restore the scene","Sterilize equipment"],1,"Gross decon removes the bulk of contamination fast, usually with copious water, before technical decon.","NFPA 470 / IFSTA Hazmat"),n("f215","ff2","ems","In a mass-casualty incident using START triage, a patient who is NOT breathing after airway repositioning is tagged:",["Red (immediate)","Yellow (delayed)","Green (minor)","Black (expectant/deceased)"],3,"Under START, no breathing after one airway repositioning means black.","START triage"),n("f216","ff2","wildland","When attacking a wildland fire, crews generally work from a(n):",["Head of the fire, directly","Anchor point, then flank the fire","Random point","Top of a chimney"],1,"Working from an anchor point (a road or burned area) keeps crews from being outflanked. Head attacks are the most dangerous.","NWCG / IFSTA Wildland"),n("f217","ff2","marine","A boat fire at a crowded dock should be prioritized to prevent:",["Smoke in the parking lot","Extension to adjacent vessels and the fuel dock","Damage to the gangway","Boat drift downstream only"],1,"Tightly packed vessels and fuel are the major exposure problem. Protect exposures and consider moving or cutting loose adjacent boats.","NFPA 303 / Marina firefighting"),n("f218","ff2","elevator","Elevator occupants who are calm and uninjured should generally be:",["Removed through the roof hatch first","Kept informed and left in the car until it can be safely leveled or a mechanic arrives, unless conditions warrant rescue","Told to pry the doors","Moved via ladder from outside"],1,"Stuck-car calls are low risk. The rescue method should match the urgency, and the safest approach is usually best.","ASME A17.4 / Technical rescue"),n("o01","officer","sizeup","A first-arriving officer's brief initial report should include:",["Only 'on scene'","Unit ID, building description, obvious conditions, actions being taken, and command status","The names of all crew members","The estimated property loss"],1,"A concise BIR sets the tone: unit, size/occupancy/construction, conditions, action plan, and command.","IFSTA Fire Officer / NFPA 1021"),n("o02","officer","sizeup","The first-arriving company officer can use which three command options?",["Investigative ('nothing showing'), fast-attack (mobile command), and command","Offensive, defensive, marginal","Primary, secondary, tertiary","Single, unified, area"],0,"IFSTA describes nothing-showing/investigative, fast-attack, and command modes for the initial officer.","IFSTA Fire Officer"),n("o03","officer","strategy","Incident priorities, in order, are:",["Property conservation, life safety, incident stabilization","Life safety, incident stabilization, property conservation","Incident stabilization, life safety, property conservation","Life safety, property conservation, incident stabilization"],1,"Life safety comes first, then stabilizing the incident, then conserving property.","IFSTA Fire Officer / NIMS"),n("o04","officer","strategy","RECEO-VS stands for:",["Rescue, Exposures, Confinement, Extinguishment, Overhaul, Ventilation, Salvage","Respond, Evaluate, Command, Engage, Operate, Ventilate, Secure","Rescue, Evacuate, Contain, Extinguish, Open, Vent, Search","Recon, Entry, Control, Exit, Overhaul, Vent, Search"],0,"This is Layman's classic tactical priority model. Ventilation and salvage are applied wherever needed.","IFSTA Fire Officer"),n("o05","officer","ics","Under common ICS geography, Side 'A' of a building is:",["The rear","The address/front side","The left side","The roof"],1,"A is the address side. B, C, and D follow clockwise. Floors are Divisions (Division 2 = second floor).","NIMS / NFPA 1561"),n("o06","officer","ics","What is the difference between a Division and a Group in ICS?",["There is none","Divisions are geographic areas; groups are functional assignments","Groups are geographic; divisions are functional","Divisions are for EMS only"],1,"Division 2 is a place. A ventilation group is a function.","NIMS / NFPA 1561"),n("o07","officer","ics","The recommended span of control in ICS is:",["1 supervisor to 3\u20137 subordinates, optimally 5","1 to 10\u201312","1 to 2","Unlimited"],0,"3\u20137 with 5 as the optimum keeps supervision manageable.","NIMS"),n("o08","officer","ric","A Rapid Intervention Crew should be in place:",["Only on second alarms","Whenever members are operating in an IDLH atmosphere, starting with the initial two-out","Only after a Mayday","Only at commercial fires"],1,"Two-out covers the initial stage, and a dedicated RIC is assigned as resources arrive.","NFPA 1407 / OSHA 1910.134"),n("o09","officer","ric","PAR stands for:",["Personnel Accountability Report","Primary Area Recon","Pump And Roll","Pre-Arrival Report"],0,"A PAR confirms that all members of each company are accounted for.","NFPA 1561"),n("o10","officer","ric","Which event should ALWAYS trigger a PAR?",["A crew taking a water break","A change from offensive to defensive strategy","The arrival of the utility company","Press arrival"],1,"Changes in strategy, evacuations, Maydays, missing members, and time benchmarks all call for a PAR.","NFPA 1561 / IFSTA"),n("o11","officer","leadership","The usual first formal step in progressive discipline is:",["Suspension","Termination","Oral reprimand / documented counseling","Demotion"],2,"Progressive discipline moves from oral reprimand to written reprimand, suspension, and then termination.","IFSTA Fire Officer \u2014 Human Resources"),n("o12","officer","ics","When command is transferred, the preferred method is:",["By text message","Face-to-face with a situation briefing, then announced on the radio","Silently, without notification","By the dispatcher"],1,"A face-to-face transfer with a full briefing keeps continuity. The transfer is then announced to all units.","NFPA 1561 / IFSTA"),n("o13","officer","strategy","Which statement reflects the accepted fire service risk-management model?",["Risk everything for property","Risk a lot within a structured plan for savable lives; risk little for savable property; risk nothing for what's already lost","Never take any risk","Risk equally for lives and property"],1,"This is the NFPA 1500 risk-management principle.","NFPA 1500 (now NFPA 1550)"),n("o14","officer","mayday","LUNAR in a Mayday transmission stands for:",["Location, Unit, Name, Assignment/Air, Resources needed","Look, Understand, Notify, Assess, React","Ladder, Utility, Nozzle, Air, Rope","Lost, Unconscious, Needs, Air, Rescue"],0,"LUNAR gives the IC and RIC what they need to find the member.","NFPA 1407 / IFSTA"),n("o15","officer","construction","A major hazard in garden apartment fires is:",["Steel bar joists","An open common cockloft or attic that allows rapid horizontal spread","Heavy timber floors","Underground parking"],1,"Common attics let fire run the whole building. Get ahead of it with ceilings opened and lines in the attic.","IFSTA Building Construction"),n("o16","officer","construction","In strip malls, fire commonly spreads between occupancies through:",["Concrete floors","Shared cocklofts and hidden roof voids (e.g., rain roofs)","Sprinkler piping","Parking lots"],1,"Common cocklofts and hidden voids above drop ceilings carry fire to adjacent stores.","IFSTA Building Construction"),n("o17","officer","hose","For a well-involved commercial occupancy, the minimum attack line is typically:",["1-inch booster","1\xbd-inch preconnect","2\xbd-inch handline","Garden hose"],2,"Large open areas and heavy fire loads call for the gpm of a 2\xbd-inch line.","IFSTA Fire Officer / Engine company ops"),n("o18","officer","safety","Crew integrity means that company members:",["Can freelance when needed","Stay together, under the direct supervision of the officer, with radio contact","Work alone to cover more area","Only meet at the engine"],1,"Crews enter together, stay together, and come out together.","NFPA 1500 / IFSTA"),n("o19","officer","mva","At a highway incident, apparatus should be positioned to:",["Park in front of the scene","Block upstream of the scene to create a protected work area","Stay on the shoulder only","Park in the median"],1,"Blocking with apparatus upstream protects crews from traffic, consistent with Traffic Incident Management practice.","NFPA 1091 / MUTCD / TIM"),n("c01","chief","ics","Unified Command is used when:",["Only one agency responds","Multiple agencies or jurisdictions with legal responsibility share incident management","The chief is off duty","Mutual aid is not needed"],1,"Under UC, agency leaders jointly set objectives through a single IAP.","NIMS"),n("c02","chief","ics","Level I staging means later-arriving units:",["Report directly to the fire","Stop at their last uncommitted position, about a block away in their direction of travel, until assigned","Go to a designated staging area with a Staging Area Manager","Return to quarters"],1,"Level I is used early. Level II uses a designated staging area and manager for larger incidents.","IFSTA / NIMS"),n("c03","chief","ics","Under NIMS, a written Incident Action Plan is generally required when an incident:",["Has any injury","Spans multiple operational periods","Involves a single engine","Is a car fire"],1,"Incidents lasting more than one operational period, along with most hazmat incidents, call for a written IAP.","NIMS"),n("c04","chief","strategy","The minimum recommended collapse zone is:",["Equal to the building width","1\xbd times the height of the wall/building","50 feet regardless","Twice the building length"],1,"1.5\xd7 height is the minimum. Mark the zone and enforce it.","IFSTA Building Construction / Fire Officer"),n("c05","chief","strategy","Which is a dangerous mixed-strategy error?",["Flowing master streams into the building while crews remain interior","Placing a RIC","Setting up rehab","Assigning a safety officer"],0,"Opposing or defensive streams can push fire and steam onto interior crews. Strategies must not be mixed in the same area.","IFSTA Fire Officer"),n("c06","chief","safety","The Incident Safety Officer has authority to:",["Override every IC decision","Alter, suspend, or terminate activities posing an imminent hazard, then inform the IC","Order evidence collection","Release units from the incident"],1,"The ISO may stop unsafe acts immediately and then notifies the IC.","NFPA 1521 (now NFPA 1550)"),n("c07","chief","safety","Under NFPA rehab guidance, members should report to rehab after using:",["One 30-minute cylinder","Two 30-minute cylinders, one 45/60-minute cylinder, or 40 minutes of intense work","Only when injured","Every 2 hours"],1,"These are the rehab triggers for heat stress and fatigue.","NFPA 1584"),n("c08","chief","strategy","Under NFPA 1720, a suburban volunteer department's goal is to assemble:",["4 members in 4 minutes","10 members within 10 minutes, 80% of the time","15 members within 9 minutes, 90% of the time","6 members within 14 minutes"],1,"Suburban areas (500\u20131,000 people/sq mi) target 10 staff in 10 minutes, 80% of the time. Urban areas target 15 in 9 minutes at 90%.","NFPA 1720"),n("c09","chief","strategy","NFPA 1710 sets the travel-time goal for the first-arriving engine at:",["240 seconds","320 seconds","480 seconds","600 seconds"],0,"That is 240 seconds of travel for the first engine, met 90% of the time, for career departments.","NFPA 1710"),n("c10","chief","strategy","Automatic aid differs from mutual aid because it is:",["Requested by the IC after arrival","Dispatched automatically under a pre-arranged agreement","Only for EMS","Not reimbursable"],1,"Automatic aid goes out on the initial dispatch. Mutual aid is requested as needed.","NFPA 1561 / NIMS"),n("c11","chief","mayday","After acknowledging a Mayday and deploying RIC, the IC should next:",["Return to normal radio traffic","Request an additional alarm/RIC, conduct a PAR, and keep fire control in place","Pull all crews out immediately","Take over the RIC personally"],1,"Maydays consume resources. Replace the RIC, keep water on the fire to protect the trapped member, and account for everyone.","NFPA 1407 / IFSTA"),n("c12","chief","ics","The command post is best located:",["Inside the lobby","In a visible, accessible spot outside the hot zone, ideally seeing two sides of the building","At the hydrant","A mile away"],1,"Corner positions on sides A/B or A/D give a two-sided view and safe access.","IFSTA Fire Officer / Command"),n("c13","chief","strategy","Switching to a defensive strategy is most justified when:",["Water supply is established","No savable lives remain and the structure is compromised or untenable","The first ladder arrives","Smoke is visible"],1,"Defensive operations protect firefighters when risk exceeds benefit.","NFPA 1500 risk management / IFSTA"),n("c14","chief","njcode","New Jersey's Incident Management System requirements for fire departments are found in:",["N.J.A.C. 5:75","N.J.S.A. 39:4","NFPA 72","N.J.A.C. 7:26"],0,"N.J.A.C. 5:75 (Fire Service Safety \u2014 Incident Management System) is the NJ rule mandating IMS use.","N.J.A.C. 5:75 (NJDFS)"),n("c15","chief","njcode","New Jersey firefighter training and certification rules are administered by the Division of Fire Safety under:",["N.J.A.C. 5:73","N.J.A.C. 5:23 (UCC only)","OSHA 1926","NFPA 101"],0,"N.J.A.C. 5:73 covers fire service training and certification.","N.J.A.C. 5:73 (NJDFS)"),n("c16","chief","strategy","A Post-Incident Analysis (PIA) should primarily be:",["A disciplinary hearing","A non-punitive review of what worked, what didn't, and lessons learned","A press conference","An insurance claim"],1,"PIAs improve future operations. Keeping them non-punitive encourages honest input.","NFPA 1561 / IFSTA"),n("c17","chief","hazmat","At a hazmat incident, the 'hot zone' is the area:",["Where the command post sits","Immediately around the release where contamination exists","Where decon occurs","Outside the perimeter"],1,"Hot = contamination, warm = decon corridor, cold = support and command.","NFPA 470 / IFSTA Hazmat"),n("c18","chief","wildland","In the wildland-urban interface, structure triage classifies a home that cannot be safely defended as:",["Defensible / prep and hold","Non-defensible (bypass/ do not commit resources)","Stand-alone","Priority one"],1,"Committing crews to non-defensible structures puts them at unacceptable risk.","NWCG WUI Structure Triage")],o=[...s,...t.GENERATED_QUESTIONS],l=new Map(o.map(t=>[t.id,t])),c=t=>l.get(t),h=t=>t.startsWith("g-")},837,[862]);
